    # Stamen & Anther (1-10)
    ("A typical angiosperm anther is bilobed and each lobe has two thecae, meaning it is:", ["Dithecous", "Monothecous", "Polithecous", "Athecous"], 0, T1, "Anther Morphology", "Dithecous nature of angiosperm anthers in NCERT.", "In typical angiosperms, each anther has two lobes, and each lobe contains two microsporangia separated by a longitudinal groove. Therefore, it is dithecous and tetrasporangiate.", "Monothecous anthers have only a single lobe (e.g., Malvaceae). Polithecous and athecous are non-standard terms."),
    ("The terminal generally bilobed fertile part of a stamen is called:", ["Anther", "Filament", "Petal", "Connective"], 0, T1, "Stamen Morphology", "Anatomy of stamen in angiosperms.", "A typical stamen consists of two parts: the long and slender stalk called the filament, and the terminal, generally bilobed structure called the anther.", "Filament is the sterile stalk. Petal is an accessory floral whorl. Connective is the sterile tissue joining anther lobes."),
    ("The proximal end of the stamen filament is attached to the:", ["Thalamus or petal", "Anther", "Stigma", "Ovary"], 0, T1, "Stamen Attachment", "Attachment points of stamen filament in NCERT.", "The proximal end of the filament is attached to the thalamus or the petal of the flower, while the distal end bears the fertile anther.", "The distal end bears the anther. Stigma and ovary are parts of the pistil/gynoecium, not the stamen attachment site."),
    ("Which of the following wall layers of a microsporangium provides nourishment to developing pollen grains?", ["Tapetum", "Endothecium", "Middle layers", "Epidermis"], 0, T1, "Microsporangium Layers", "Nutritive function of the tapetum.", "A microsporangium is surrounded by four wall layers: Epidermis, Endothecium, Middle layers, and Tapetum. The innermost tapetum nourishes the developing microspores/pollen grains.", "Epidermis, endothecium, and middle layers perform mechanical protection and dehiscence to release pollen grains."),
    ("The cells of which microsporangial wall layer possess dense cytoplasm and are generally multinucleate?", ["Tapetum", "Epidermis", "Endothecium", "Middle layer"], 0, T1, "Tapetum Cytology", "Cytological features of tapetal cells in NCERT.", "Cells of the tapetum possess dense cytoplasm and frequently undergo endomitosis without cytokinesis, resulting in a multinucleate, polyploid state.", "Cells of the epidermis, endothecium, and middle layers remain uninucleate diploid cells with standard vacuolation."),
    ("The outer three wall layers of a microsporangium collectively perform the function of:", ["Protection and dehiscence of anther", "Nutrition of pollen grains", "Production of sporopollenin", "Formation of gametes"], 0, T1, "Anther Dehiscence", "Function of outer three anther wall layers.", "The outer three wall layers (epidermis, endothecium, and middle layers) protect the internal sporogenous tissue and assist in anther dehiscence to release mature pollen.", "Nutrition is the exclusive role of tapetum. Sporopollenin is synthesized by tapetum and pollen. Gamete formation occurs inside pollen."),
    ("In an anther, the fibrous thickenings of alpha-cellulosic bands are characteristic of:", ["Endothecium", "Tapetum", "Epidermis", "Middle layers"], 0, T1, "Endothecium Structure", "Fibrous thickenings in endothecial cells.", "Endothecial cells develop characteristic hygroscopic alpha-cellulosic fibrous bands along their radial and inner tangential walls, facilitating anther dehiscence upon drying.", "Tapetum is nutritive without fibrous bands. Epidermis is protective. Middle layers are ephemeral and degenerate before maturity."),
    ("The tissue occupying the center of each microsporangium in a young anther is called:", ["Sporogenous tissue", "Tapetum", "Nucellus", "Vascular bundle"], 0, T1, "Sporogenous Tissue", "Sporogenous tissue in young anther.", "When the anther is young, a group of compactly arranged homogenous cells called the sporogenous tissue occupies the center of each microsporangium.", "Tapetum surrounds the sporogenous tissue. Nucellus is present inside the ovule. Vascular bundle is located in the central connective."),
    ("The process of formation of microspores from a pollen mother cell (PMC) through meiosis is called:", ["Microsporogenesis", "Megasporogenesis", "Spermatogenesis", "Microgametogenesis"], 0, T1, "Microsporogenesis", "Definition of microsporogenesis in NCERT.", "As the anther develops, each cell of the sporogenous tissue undergoes meiotic reduction division to give rise to microspore tetrads. This process is microsporogenesis.", "Megasporogenesis produces megaspores in ovules. Spermatogenesis is animal sperm formation. Microgametogenesis is gamete formation inside pollen."),
    ("The ploidy of a pollen mother cell (PMC) and a microspore in an angiosperm is respectively:", ["2n and n", "n and 2n", "2n and 2n", "n and n"], 0, T1, "Ploidy Levels", "Ploidy transition during microsporogenesis.", "The pollen mother cell (PMC) is a diploid (2n) sporophytic cell that undergoes meiosis to form haploid (n) microspores arranged in a tetrad.", "PMC is diploid (2n), not haploid. Microspores are haploid (n), formed after meiotic reduction division."),

    # Pollen Grain Architecture & Cytology (11-25)
    ("The outer hard layer of pollen grain wall called exine is composed of:", ["Sporopollenin", "Pectocellulose", "Lignocellulose", "Chitin"], 0, T1, "Pollen Wall", "Sporopollenin composition of pollen exine.", "The exine is composed of sporopollenin, one of the most resistant organic substances known. It withstands high temperatures, strong acids, and alkali.", "Pectocellulose forms the intine. Lignocellulose forms xylem walls. Chitin forms fungal cell walls and insect exoskeletons."),
    ("No enzyme that degrades which of the following substances is so far known?", ["Sporopollenin", "Cellulose", "Pectin", "Lignin"], 0, T1, "Sporopollenin Resistance", "Chemical resistance of sporopollenin in NCERT.", "Sporopollenin is extremely resistant to chemical and biological degradation; no known enzyme can digest it, allowing pollen grains to be preserved well as microfossils.", "Cellulase degrades cellulose. Pectinase degrades pectin. Laccase/peroxidase degrades lignin."),
    ("Pollen grains are well preserved as fossils because of the presence of:", ["Sporopollenin in exine", "Cellulose in intine", "Lipids in vegetative cell", "Proteins in generative cell"], 0, T1, "Pollen Fossils", "Fossil preservation of pollen grains.", "The presence of chemically inert and highly durable sporopollenin in the exine enables pollen grains to remain preserved in sediments for millions of years.", "Intine cellulose, vegetative lipids, and generative proteins degrade rapidly after cell death."),
    ("Germ pores are apertures on the pollen exine where:", ["Sporopollenin is absent", "Intine is completely missing", "Both exine and intine are absent", "Sporopollenin is doubly thickened"], 0, T1, "Germ Pores", "Germ pore structure on pollen grain.", "Germ pores are circular apertures on the exine where sporopollenin deposition is absent, permitting the underlying intine to push out as the pollen tube upon germination.", "Intine is continuous and present across the germ pore. Sporopollenin is absent, not thickened."),
    ("The inner wall layer of the pollen grain is called intine and is composed of:", ["Cellulose and pectin", "Sporopollenin and cutin", "Chitin and lignin", "Suberin and wax"], 0, T1, "Intine Composition", "Chemical composition of intine.", "The intine is a thin, uniform, continuous inner layer composed of cellulose and pectin. It forms the primary wall of the elongating pollen tube.", "Sporopollenin is found strictly in the exine. Chitin is fungal. Suberin is found in Casparian strips and cork."),
    ("In a mature 2-celled pollen grain, the vegetative cell is characterized by:", ["Abundant food reserves and a large irregularly shaped nucleus", "Spindle shape with dense cytoplasm and no nucleus", "Small size floating in generative cytoplasm", "Lack of vacuole and haploid chromosome deficiency"], 0, T1, "Vegetative Cell", "Cytological characteristics of the vegetative cell.", "The vegetative cell is bigger, has abundant food reserve, and contains a large, irregularly shaped nucleus, providing nutrients for tube growth.", "The generative cell is small and spindle-shaped. The vegetative cell has a large nucleus and prominent vacuoles."),
    ("The generative cell of an angiosperm pollen grain is:", ["Small, spindle-shaped with dense cytoplasm and a nucleus", "Large, spherical with abundant starch grains", "Devoid of a nucleus and strictly nutritive", "Located outside the intine wall"], 0, T1, "Generative Cell", "Morphology of the generative cell.", "The generative cell is small, spindle-shaped with dense cytoplasm and a prominent nucleus, and floats in the cytoplasm of the larger vegetative cell.", "Vegetative cell is large with abundant starch/food. Generative cell is nucleated and located inside the intine."),
    ("In over 60% of angiosperms, pollen grains are shed from the anther at the:", ["2-celled stage", "3-celled stage", "4-celled stage", "1-celled stage"], 0, T1, "Shedding Stage", "Pollen shedding stage in majority of angiosperms.", "In over 60 percent of angiosperms, pollen grains are shed at the 2-celled stage (one vegetative cell and one generative cell). In <40%, generative cell divides before shedding.", "3-celled shedding occurs in remaining species (<40%). 1-celled and 4-celled shedding do not occur in typical angiosperms."),
    ("In angiosperms shedding pollen at the 3-celled stage, the generative cell divides to form:", ["Two male gametes", "Two vegetative cells", "One male gamete and one tube nucleus", "Three male gametes"], 0, T1, "Male Gametes", "Mitotic division of generative cell.", "The generative cell divides mitotically to produce two non-motile male gametes either prior to anther dehiscence (3-celled) or inside the pollen tube on the stigma (2-celled).", "Vegetative cell does not divide again. Two male gametes are formed, not one or three."),
    ("Pollen allergy in India became widespread due to the introduction of which weed as a wheat contaminant?", ["Parthenium hysterophorus", "Eichhornia crassipes", "Lantana camara", "Pistia stratiotes"], 0, T1, "Pollen Allergy", "Parthenium contamination and pollen allergy.", "Parthenium or carrot grass came into India as a contaminant with imported wheat and has become ubiquitous, causing widespread allergic rhinitis, asthma, and bronchitis.", "Eichhornia is water hyacinth (Terror of Bengal). Lantana is an invasive terrestrial shrub. Pistia is water lettuce."),

