    ("Pollen grains maintain viability for several months in members of which plant families?", ["Rosaceae, Leguminosae, and Solanaceae", "Poaceae, Gramineae, and Cyperaceae", "Brassicaceae, Cruciferae, and Orchidaceae", "Asteraceae, Malvaceae, and Liliaceae"], 0, T1, "Long Pollen Viability", "Plant families with extended pollen viability.", "According to NCERT, in some members of Rosaceae, Leguminosae, and Solanaceae, pollen grains maintain viability for months under natural conditions.", "Poaceae pollen loses viability within 30 minutes."),
    ("Pollen grains of numerous species can be cryopreserved for years in pollen banks using liquid nitrogen at:", ["-196°C", "-96°C", "-50°C", "0°C"], 0, T1, "Cryopreservation", "Liquid nitrogen cryopreservation temperature.", "Liquid nitrogen storage at -196°C (77 K) halts all metabolic activity, allowing long-term preservation of viable pollen for crop breeding programmes.", "Boiling point of liquid nitrogen is -196°C; other listed temperatures do not achieve cryogenic vitrification."),
    ("A gynoecium consisting of multiple pistils fused together into a single unit is described as:", ["Syncarpous", "Apocarpous", "Monocarpellary", "Polyadelphous"], 0, T1, "Gynoecium Types", "Syncarpous gynoecium definition.", "When a flower possesses multiple carpels fused together, the gynoecium is called syncarpous (e.g., Papaver somniferum).", "Apocarpous gynoecium has free carpels (e.g., Michelia). Monocarpellary has a single carpel. Polyadelphous refers to stamens."),
