    ("Which of the following plants is a classic NCERT example of a multicarpellary, syncarpous pistil?", ["Papaver", "Michelia", "Lotus", "Rose"], 0, T1, "Syncarpous Example", "Papaver as multicarpellary syncarpous flower.", "Papaver (poppy) possesses a multicarpellary syncarpous pistil with fused carpels forming a single compound capsule.", "Michelia, Lotus, and Rose possess apocarpous gynoecia with distinct free carpels."),
    ("The basal swollen part of the pistil containing one or more ovules is the:", ["Ovary", "Stigma", "Style", "Thalamus"], 0, T1, "Pistil Morphology", "Ovary anatomy in angiosperms.", "The pistil has three parts: stigma (pollen landing platform), style (elongated slender stalk), and the basal swollen part called the ovary.", "Stigma is apical. Style is the neck. Thalamus is the floral receptacle supporting the floral whorls."),
    ("The landing platform for pollen grains on the female reproductive organ is the:", ["Stigma", "Style", "Ovary", "Placenta"], 0, T1, "Stigma Function", "Role of stigma in pollination.", "The stigma serves as the receptive landing platform where pollen grains land during pollination and undergo hydration and germination.", "Style conducts the pollen tube. Ovary houses ovules. Placenta attaches ovules to the ovary wall."),
    ("Inside the ovary is the ovarian cavity (locule), on which ovules are attached by the:", ["Placenta", "Connective", "Endosperm", "Funicle alone"], 0, T1, "Placentation", "Placenta and locule in angiosperms.", "Inside the ovarian cavity (locule) is the placenta, from which megasporangia (commonly called ovules) arise via a stalk called the funicle.", "Connective joins anther lobes. Endosperm is post-fertilisation nutritive tissue inside the seed."),

